<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spartacist uprising</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as January uprising</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spartacist uprising was a general strike and armed rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> from 5 to 12 January 1919, led by the newly founded Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and other radicals against the Social Democratic government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a>. It was crushed by army units and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-noske">Gustav Noske</a>, and the communist leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a> and Karl Liebknecht were murdered on 15 January.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising.jpg" alt="Spartacist uprising" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: New-York Tribune · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soldiers_the_Brandenburger_Tor_during_the_Spartacist_uprising_Jan_7_1919.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 10 November 1918, after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, a Council of the People's Deputies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-ebert">Friedrich Ebert</a> of the Majority Social Democratic Party (MSPD) and Hugo Haase of the more left-wing Independent Social Democratic Party (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/independent-social-democratic-party-of-germany">USPD</a>) formed a provisional government. In the secret Ebert-Groener Pact the army command promised its loyalty, and Ebert promised prompt action against leftist uprisings. The MSPD sought elections to a constituent assembly, while the USPD and part of the working class wanted a council republic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a> and Karl Liebknecht founded the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> (KPD) on 1 January 1919. The dismissal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>'s police chief Emil Eichhorn on 4 January was the immediate trigger.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A protest on 5 January drew 100,000 people, and armed demonstrators occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> plants of the newspaper Vorwärts and the Berliner Tageblatt. That evening about 70 leaders voted to form a revolutionary committee to overthrow the government. Up to 500,000 people answered its call for a general strike on 7 January, but the committee could not agree on how to proceed and the crowds went home. Negotiations with Ebert failed over the occupied buildings and Eichhorn's reinstatement.</p><p>Ebert gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-noske">Gustav Noske</a> command of the troops around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> units formed from former frontline soldiers were mustered. On 11 January the Vorwärts building was taken with flame throwers, machine guns, mortars and artillery. Many insurgents surrendered, and seven men who came to negotiate were taken to the Dragoon Barracks and shot. An investigative committee of the Prussian parliament put the death toll at 156.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On the evening of 15 January Luxemburg and Liebknecht were arrested in Wilmersdorf and taken to the Eden Hotel, where Captain Waldemar Pabst had them interrogated and beaten. Luxemburg was shot in the temple and thrown into the Landwehr Canal; Liebknecht was shot in the Tiergarten as a supposed fugitive. At the trial in May 1919 Otto Runge received two years in prison and Kurt Vogel 28 months, and Pabst was not charged. Elections to the National Assembly were held on 19 January, and it finalised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-constitution">Weimar Constitution</a>. The MSPD received 37.9 per cent, and its part in the suppression weighed on it throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spartacist uprising” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Early violence was heavy: fighting with the People's Navy Division in December 1918 was followed by the <b>Spartacist uprising</b> of 5–12 January 1919, with an estimated 150 to 200 dead, strikes of 350,000 Ruhr miners and the first Silesian uprising of August 1919, put down by military means.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1919 the <b>Spartacist uprising</b> was crushed by Freikorps units, and Rosa Luxemburg and Karl Liebknecht were killed after their arrests.</blockquote><a href="/empire/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the defeat of the <b>Spartacist uprising</b>, the two communist leaders were seized and killed by Freikorps soldiers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-15">15 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On 10 January 1919, after the <b>Spartacist rising</b> in Berlin, left-wing radicals and Communists proclaimed a Soviet Republic, dissolving the city's senate and parliament.</blockquote><a href="/state/bremen-soviet-republic">Bremen Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
